````

### One important thing for your project

Tumhare structure mein:

```text
data/
├── raw/
├── processed/
└── sample/
````

**`sample/` ko GitHub par rakhna chahiye**, because SIH judges/team members ko small sample data se project run/test karna useful hoga.

So don't ignore:

```text
data/sample/
```

Similarly, ye files GitHub mein **keep** karo:

```text
README.md
requirements.txt
.env.example
data/sample/sample_anomalies.csv
gis/layers/*.geojson
ml/notebooks/model_training.ipynb
```

But actual:

```text
.env
data/raw/
large satellite images
trained *.pkl models
large processed datasets
```

GitHub mein nahi daalne chahiye.

### ⚠️ `.env.example` vs `.env`

Keep:

```text
.env.example
```

Ignore:

```text
.env
```

For example `.env.example`:

```env
FIRMS_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
DATABASE_URL=your_database_url_here
```

Actual `.env`:

```env
FIRMS_API_KEY=abc123_actual_secret
DATABASE_URL=actual_database_password
```

Actual secret **kabhi GitHub par push mat karna**.

### Check karne ke liye

Project root mein:

```powershell
git status
```

Aur agar `.env` accidentally tracked ho gaya ho:

```powershell
git rm --cached .env
```

Phir:

```powershell
git status
```

Is project ke liye **`.gitignore ka main principle** ye rakho:

```text
SOURCE CODE       → GitHub ✅
CONFIG TEMPLATE   → GitHub ✅
SAMPLE DATA       → GitHub ✅
DOCUMENTATION     → GitHub ✅

API KEYS          → GitHub ❌
.env              → GitHub ❌
VIRTUAL ENV       → GitHub ❌
RAW SATELLITE     → GitHub ❌
LARGE DATASETS    → GitHub ❌
TRAINED MODELS    → GitHub ❌
CACHE/TEMP FILES  → GitHub ❌
```
